const e="2026-11-10",a=[{title:"A Planet Running Hot: 2026 Set to Rank Among the Warmest Years on Record",source:"World Meteorological Organization, State of the Global Climate Update 2026 (via phys.org / scimex.org)",passage:`On 9 November 2026, as world leaders gathered in Antalya, Turkiye, for the opening of COP31, the annual United Nations climate conference, the World Meteorological Organization (WMO) delivered a sobering assessment: the year 2026 is set to rank among the four hottest years ever recorded. The WMO's State of the Global Climate Update 2026 shows that the global average surface temperature for January to September stood about 1.47 degrees Celsius above the pre-industrial average, a figure likely to rise further before the year ends. The past twelve years have individually been the warmest on record, with the past four occupying the top four positions - a streak that scientists describe as unprecedented in the instrumental record.

A major driver of the additional heat is a very strong El Nino event, which has been intensifying since August and is expected to become the strongest on record by December. El Nino, a periodic warming of surface waters in the equatorial Pacific, is forecast to persist into early 2027, with the WMO putting the probability of its continuation through February 2027 at near 100 percent. The phenomenon acts as a risk multiplier: increased heat, drought and heavy rainfall can contribute to heat-related illness and mortality, wildfire smoke, malnutrition and food insecurity, and changing patterns of water- and vector-borne disease. The World Health Organization has, for the first time, classified the anticipated health impacts of this El Nino as a health emergency.

The human cost of the year's extreme weather is already stark. Across Western Europe, heatwaves, drought and wildfires led to more than 30,000 excess deaths according to preliminary estimates, with national temperature records broken in at least eight countries and major disruption to agriculture, energy and transport. In India, an estimated 146.5 million people were exposed to extreme heat during a heatwave in April 2026. In late August, a glacier collapse in the Himalayas triggered a catastrophic flash flood downstream that killed more than 1,300 people and inflicted massive damage on Nepal's economy. Such events, the WMO notes, have undermined food security, deepened poverty and contributed to displacement in multiple regions.

Not all assessments agree on the precise ranking. Berkeley Earth, an independent American climate research group, has said it is 95 percent confident that 2026 will prove the hottest year in recorded history, surpassing the previous record set in 2024, when the global average was 1.55 degrees above the pre-industrial baseline. The WMO's more cautious position - among the top four - reflects the fact that its update draws on data only up to September and therefore does not capture the most recent effects of El Nino. Its final report for 2026 will not be issued until March 2027.

The policy response, meanwhile, remains far short of what scientists say is required. A report by the United Nations Environment Programme published in October found that the world is already poised to overshoot the 1.5-degree threshold set under the 2015 Paris Agreement, and is currently on track for approximately 2.8 degrees of warming above pre-industrial levels by 2100. "Leaders must get serious by cutting emissions faster, accelerating a just transition from fossil fuels and delivering the finance and protection vulnerable countries need," United Nations Secretary-General Antonio Guterres said at the opening of the conference. The COP31 presidency has set a goal of electrifying more than a third of the global economy by 2035, up from around a fifth today - an ambition that would require a rapid expansion of clean power, grids and electric transport. Whether the talks in Antalya can convert such ambitions into binding commitments will determine, in large measure, how much hotter the coming years become.`,notes:[{word:"unprecedented",pos:"adj.",meaning:"前所未有的；空前的"},{word:"sobering",pos:"adj.",meaning:"令人警醒的；发人深省的"},{word:"anomaly",pos:"n.",meaning:"异常；反常现象"},{word:"intensify",pos:"v.",meaning:"加剧；增强"},{word:"compound",pos:"v.",meaning:"使恶化；加剧"},{word:"vulnerable",pos:"adj.",meaning:"脆弱的；易受伤害的"},{word:"preliminary",pos:"adj.",meaning:"初步的；预备的"},{word:"excess deaths",pos:"n. phrase",meaning:"超额死亡人数"},{word:"displacement",pos:"n.",meaning:"流离失所；被迫迁移"},{word:"threshold",pos:"n.",meaning:"阈值；临界点"},{word:"trajectory",pos:"n.",meaning:"轨迹；发展趋势"},{word:"exacerbate",pos:"v.",meaning:"恶化；加重"},{word:"glacier",pos:"n.",meaning:"冰川"},{word:"emission",pos:"n.",meaning:"排放（物）"},{word:"resilience",pos:"n.",meaning:"恢复力；适应力"}],questions:[{q:"According to the WMO update, how did global temperatures in January-September 2026 compare with the pre-industrial average?",options:["About 1.55 degrees Celsius above it.","About 1.47 degrees Celsius above it.","About 1.43 degrees Celsius above it.","About 2.8 degrees Celsius above it."],answer:1},{q:"Why is the WMO's ranking of 2026 more cautious than Berkeley Earth's?",options:["The WMO uses a different definition of the pre-industrial baseline.","The WMO's update only includes data up to September 2026.","The WMO excludes the effects of El Nino from its calculations.","The WMO's final report will not be published until 2027."],answer:1},{q:"The El Nino event is expected to weaken before the end of 2026.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:1},{q:"Which of the following extreme events in 2026 is mentioned in the passage?",options:["A hurricane that struck the Caribbean in October.","A glacier collapse in the Himalayas that caused a deadly flash flood.","A volcanic eruption in Indonesia.","A typhoon that hit the Philippines in September."],answer:1},{q:"The WHO has classified the health impacts of the current El Nino as a health emergency for the first time.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0},{q:"According to the passage, what is the world currently on track for by 2100?",options:["Warming of about 1.5 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels.","Warming of about 2.8 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels.","A complete halt to fossil fuel use.","Electrification of more than a third of the global economy."],answer:1},{q:"What goal has the COP31 presidency set for 2035?",options:["To cut global emissions to zero.","To electrify more than a third of the global economy.","To end all fossil fuel subsidies.","To publish a final climate report every year."],answer:1}]},{title:"Back to the Moon - This Time to Stay: NASA Awards $10 Billion in Lunar Base Contracts",source:"Investor's Business Daily",passage:`NASA has taken its most decisive step yet towards a permanent human presence on the Moon, awarding more than 10 billion dollars in contracts to SpaceX, Blue Origin, Lockheed Martin and other companies to build the infrastructure of a lunar base. The agency announced the awards over the weekend during its MAX POWER aerospace expo and air show at the Kennedy Space Center in Florida, as part of a long-term plan to establish a base at the Moon's South Pole - a goal that would transform the Moon from a destination for brief visits into a place where people live and work.

At the heart of the announcement is the next phase of NASA's Commercial Lunar Payload Services (CLPS) initiative. The agency selected eight companies for a ten-year, multiple-award framework contract with a combined maximum value of 10 billion dollars. The companies - SpaceX, Blue Origin, Firefly Aerospace, Intuitive Machines, Lockheed Martin, Northrop Grumman, Voyager Technologies and ispace-U.S. - will compete for task orders covering cargo landers, nuclear-powered rovers, and launch and transfer services. The original CLPS contract will run through November 2028, with an additional five-year period to complete contracted lunar deliveries through November 2033.

One of the most ambitious awards went to Blue Origin, the space company founded by Jeff Bezos. NASA is moving forward with the development of PROMISE - the Polar Rover for Observation, Mapping and In-Situ Exploration - a six-wheeled, nuclear-powered rover designed to carry scientific instruments and conduct experiments across the rugged terrain of the lunar South Pole. The agency awarded Blue Origin a 94-million-dollar base contract, with an option worth a further 140 million dollars based on performance, to deliver the rover to the South Pole aboard its Mark 1 lander. The contract expands a task order issued in May 2026 to deliver two lunar vehicles to the Moon. NASA plans to launch PROMISE in late 2028 for an 18-month mission, during which the rover will operate through the long lunar nights that solar-powered vehicles cannot survive.

NASA also awarded six task orders for Orbital Transfer Vehicles - spacecraft that move payloads between lunar orbit and the surface - to Blue Origin, Arrow Science and Technology and Impulse Space, and selected SpaceX for a demonstration of cislunar communications, the relay network that will link the lunar surface with mission control on Earth. Together, these awards cover the full chain of a sustained lunar operation: getting there, moving around, generating power, and staying in contact.

The announcement comes as the regulatory environment for commercial spaceflight is being reshaped. The Federal Aviation Administration announced plans last week to overhaul its licensing framework next year in order to streamline commercial launches, part of a broader government effort to reach 1,000 launches a year by 2030 and accelerate to 10,000 annually by 2035. Investors responded enthusiastically: space stocks surged on Monday, continuing rallies that began late last week.

The scale of the commitment reflects how far commercial spaceflight has come. A decade ago, lunar landings were the preserve of government agencies; today, private companies design, build and fly the hardware, with NASA acting increasingly as a customer rather than an operator. Under the CLPS model, NASA buys delivery services rather than owning the spacecraft outright, a shift intended to lower costs and accelerate the pace of missions. If the model works at the Moon, the agency hopes to apply the same approach to the far more distant and demanding goal of Mars.

The choice of the lunar South Pole is no accident. Its permanently shadowed craters are believed to hold water ice, which could be converted into drinking water, oxygen and even rocket propellant, while nearby peaks enjoy near-constant sunlight for solar power. If the gamble succeeds, the Moon will become both a scientific laboratory and a proving ground for the far harder journey to Mars - a place, in NASA's phrase, where humanity learns to live off the land of another world.`,notes:[{word:"lunar",pos:"adj.",meaning:"月球的；月亮的"},{word:"infrastructure",pos:"n.",meaning:"基础设施"},{word:"payload",pos:"n.",meaning:"有效载荷；装载量"},{word:"rover",pos:"n.",meaning:"巡视器；漫游车"},{word:"terrain",pos:"n.",meaning:"地形；地势"},{word:"propellant",pos:"n.",meaning:"推进剂；燃料"},{word:"contractor",pos:"n.",meaning:"承包商"},{word:"framework",pos:"n.",meaning:"框架；体系"},{word:"milestone",pos:"n.",meaning:"里程碑；重大进展"},{word:"frontier",pos:"n.",meaning:"前沿；边疆"},{word:"deploy",pos:"v.",meaning:"部署；调动"},{word:"autonomous",pos:"adj.",meaning:"自主的；自动的"},{word:"sustainable",pos:"adj.",meaning:"可持续的"},{word:"venture",pos:"n.",meaning:"冒险事业；企业"}],questions:[{q:"What is the main purpose of NASA's contracts announced at the Kennedy Space Center?",options:["To fund a single crewed mission to the Moon.","To build the infrastructure for a long-term base at the Moon's South Pole.","To replace the International Space Station.","To launch a telescope into lunar orbit."],answer:1},{q:"How many companies were selected for the next phase of the CLPS initiative?",options:["Six","Eight","Ten","Twelve"],answer:1},{q:"The combined maximum value of the CLPS framework contract is 10 billion dollars.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0},{q:"What is special about the PROMISE rover?",options:["It is the first rover to be powered entirely by solar panels.","It is a six-wheeled, nuclear-powered rover designed for the lunar South Pole.","It will carry astronauts across the lunar surface.","It was built by SpaceX."],answer:1},{q:"PROMISE is scheduled to be launched in late 2028 for an 18-month mission.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0},{q:"According to the passage, why is the lunar South Pole an attractive location for a base?",options:["It has the flattest terrain on the Moon.","It is the closest point on the Moon to Earth.","Its shadowed craters may hold water ice and nearby peaks receive near-constant sunlight.","It was the landing site of the Apollo missions."],answer:2},{q:"What target has the US government set for commercial launches by 2035?",options:["100 launches a year.","1,000 launches a year.","10,000 launches a year.","100,000 launches a year."],answer:2}]}],t={date:e,newsReadings:a};export{e as date,t as default,a as newsReadings};
